My point was that there are always plenty of opportunities for getting
wishes, but very few chances of a permanent light source (the only
three I can think of being magic lamps, sunsword, and polymorphing into
something that glows [probably not a yellow light, though]) .  There
are also many non-wish ways of getting MR, reflection, and DSM; the
chances go up even higher if you're playing on a public server, such as
NAO, or using Hearse--my recent Valk YAAP was helped by a double bones
minetown.  Because many people instantly turn magic lamps into wishes,
most bones files don't include a magic lamp.

If you don't have a light source, mapping out a level is an exercise in
caution, patience, and luck, since you have to visit far more squares
to find the walls & random equipment, which means that you're spending
more time on each level (increasing risk of starvation), and more
likely to step on a trap.  I've played enough early games at this point
that I'm able to handle being in the dark (though I don't know that
I'll ever try a zen samurai...), but I'd still prefer to explore in the
light.

Of course, the first point where you're likely to be able to tell
whether or not a lamp is blessed (much less be able to bless it),
unless you're a priest, is minetown; my experience has been that,
generally, if I can survive to that point, I can survive back up and
down to Sokoban, etc.  Would DSM, or a BOH, or an AoR, or an artifact
help?  Probably.  Are they necessary?  Not to me, at this point--I've
learned how to deal without them when needed.  Does having a light
source help?  Definitely.  Is it necessary?  No, not really--but it
makes me much more comfortable; the easiest way for me to die is
running into something I should've been able to see coming (like a pack
of rothes--if I can see them in time, I can get to a hallway; if I
can't, I may end up having to fight them in a large room, which is not
ideal.)  If I have infravision, light's less of an issue, obviously.

And, of course, wishes feel almost like cheating to me, at least partly
because my two ascensions have both gotten a lot easier once I had the
castle wand--almost boring, in fact.  I'm wishless so far with my
current game (post-quest wizard, homemade silver DSM, most spells, down
to Medusa's island), though I must confess to having been strongly
tempted to wish up a magic marker every time I've cleared a
throneroom...

